## Changes that shouldn't matter

<At levels="simple">

Back in <ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" />, small changes in wording changed an answer. Real
prompts have many choices like that: which examples, in what order, laid out how, and where the
important part goes. None of them should matter. Here are three, each changed on its own:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Back in <ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" />, small changes in wording changed an answer. Prompts for real tasks have many
such choices: which examples, in what order, laid out how, and where the important part goes. None
of them should matter to the meaning. Here are three, each changed on its own:

</At>

<FragileLab />

## Order

<At levels="simple">

Shuffling the same four examples changed how many of eight reviews GPT-2 small labeled right: from
all 8 to just 4, no better than a coin. Much larger models do this too, and an order that works
for one model may not work for another.<Cite id="lu2022" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Shuffling the same four examples changed how many of the eight reviews GPT-2 small labeled right
from 8 of 8 to 4 of 8, which is no better than a coin. Researchers found the same effect in much
larger models: some orders do nearly as well as a fully trained classifier and others are no better
than random guessing, and a good order for one model doesn't carry over to another.<Cite id="lu2022" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Why order matters

The orders that went badly have something in common. All five that scored 4 of 8 ended with a
positive example, and all twelve that ended with a negative one scored 6 or more. Models tend to
favor labels that appear near the end of the prompt, and labels that were common in their
training text. One fix, *contextual calibration*, measures that lean directly: give the prompt a
test input with no content, like "N/A", see how far the answer tilts away from even, and correct
every real answer by the same adjustment. It raised GPT-3 and GPT-2's average accuracy by up to
30 points and made the choice of prompt matter less.<Cite id="zhao2021" />

</At>

## Layout

<At levels="simple">

Writing the examples as "Review: … Sentiment: …" got 7 of 8 right; an arrow between review and
label got 4. Formatting alone makes big differences in larger models too.<Cite id="sclar2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Writing the same examples as "Review: … Sentiment: …" got 7 of 8 right; an arrow between review and
label got 4. A study of open models found differences of up to 76 percentage points from formatting
alone, and the gap didn't go away with bigger models, more examples, or instruction
tuning.<Cite id="sclar2024" />

</At>

## Position

<At levels="simple">

With 30 lines of locker numbers, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct almost always found the right one when it
was the first or last line. Every miss came with it somewhere in the middle. Studies of large
models found the same thing.<Cite id="liu2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

With 30 lines of locker numbers, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct found the right one almost every time when
it was the first or last line. All of its 8 misses came with the line somewhere in the middle.
Large studies show the same U shape: models use information at the start and end of a long prompt
better than information in the middle.<Cite id="liu2024" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

When Qwen missed, it missed completely. In all 8 misses, the right number got under a 2% chance,
while 76 of the other 80 answers got over 99%. The model wasn't unsure about those lines; it put
nearly all its probability on some other answer.

</At>

## What to do about it

Because the model only continues text, every one of these choices is part of the input, and none of
them is neutral. Good prompting is less about magic words and more about testing: try a few
wordings, orders, and layouts on real examples, keep what works, and put what matters most near the
start or the end.

<RecapCard>

- The order of examples, their layout, and where facts sit in a long prompt can all change answers.
- These effects show up in large models too, and differ from model to model.
- Test several versions of a prompt on real examples instead of trusting one wording.

<At levels="deep">

- Labels near the end of the prompt pull answers toward them; calibration measures the pull with a
  content-free input.

</At>
</RecapCard>

